  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m use CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m and never run in the browser. There's no bundler, no test runner,
  and no dependencies in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. So "a shared module other forms can use" has no existing place to live — that's
  the main thing the design has to settle.

  First question.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "persist" can mean the identity survives a page reload on this one machine, or that the login is recorded
  somewhere you can query later. These aren't variations on one design — they're different systems, and only the second
  needs a backend.

  - [1mClient-side only[0m — store the userId in the browser (localStorage/sessionStorage/cookie) so later forms on later page
    loads can read it. No server work, ships against the existing stub, and it's genuinely reversible. But it's
    per-browser and per-device, the user can clear it, and it's not a record of who logged in — it's a cache of who's
    logged in [3mnow[0m.
  - [1mServer-side[0m — the login POST records the event and returns a session the app carries. This is what gives you an
    actual answer to "who logged in," across devices, that survives a cleared browser. It also means the stubbed
    [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m has to become real, which is a much larger scope than this repo currently has anywhere in it.
  - [1mBoth[0m — server is the record, client stores the session id so other forms can attach it. Common end state, but it's
    the server design plus extra.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m I need your answer before I can recommend, because the honest answer depends on something I can't
  see from the repo — whether a backend exists outside this checkout. If there is no backend yet, client-side is the
  only thing that can actually be built now, and the server piece becomes a separate project. If there is one, "both" is
  where you'll land.
